ers, the crash wasn’t just a market correction; it was evidence that the Internet was a classic speculative mania like tulips or the South Sea Bubble, and that the entire premise had been a collective delusion. **4. It was constantly compared to earlier overhyped flops.** Skeptics drew parallels to technologies that had similarly promised revolutionary change but stalled: CB radio was supposed to create a democratic communications network; interactive TV and videotex (like Minitel in France, though Minitel was actually a success in its own limited way) were going to bring shopping and information to living rooms. The Internet looked to many like just the next in that line of “this time it’s different” false dawns. **5. The digital divide and fear factor.** For the average person in the early-to-mid 1990s, the Internet was something they saw mentioned in magazines but had no personal access to. They heard scary stories about hackers, child predators, and unmoderated pornography. Mainstream coverage often framed it as a wild, lawless frontier that most sensible people would avoid. This made it easy to dismiss as a passing, dangerous trend rather than a permanent utility. **6. Breathtakingly wrong predictions from respected authorities.** History is littered with famous quotes that capture the era’s skepticism: - Robert Metcalfe, inventor of Ethernet, predicted in 1995 that the Internet would “catastrophically collapse” within a year. - An Astor lecture at Oxford in the early ’90s suggested it would become a “fad of the ’90s like a supermarket for pornography and gossip.” - Even Bill Gates initially downplayed the Internet’s potential in his first book, *The Road Ahead* (1995), before famously reversing course. **So why were they wrong?** The skeptics underestimated the sheer speed at which infrastructure (broadband, Wi-Fi) and interfaces (the graphical browser, the smartphone) would improve, making the Internet invisible and indispensable. They couldn’t foresee entirely new business models (search advertising, subscription streaming, the app economy) or the rise of social media. And they misjudged how fundamentally the network would invade the physical world—cars, homes, pockets, and now intelligence itself. In short, the 1990s skepticism wasn’t just cynicism; it was a reaction to a technology that, in its early form, really did look like a slow, impractical, money-losing experiment. The fact that it evolved into the backbone of modern life was a plot twist that even many of its staunchest early believers didn’t fully see coming.
